FIT_MORE_18 = [

('hyrox-explained', 'Understand', 'Hyrox, explained: the race format behind the fastest-growing fitness sport', 'What the eight kilometres and eight stations actually are, why the fixed format made it grow from 650 to over a million athletes, and what training for it looks like.', '<p>Hyrox calls itself fitness racing, and the pitch is simple enough to explain in one paragraph: an 8 km run, chopped into eight 1 km laps, with a functional workout station after every lap. Same course, same weights, same distances, everywhere in the world &mdash; which turns it into something most fitness events are not: a time you can genuinely compare.</p>\n<h2>The format, in full</h2>\n<p>Every race is identical: 1 km of running, then a station, eight times over. The stations, in order, are 1,000 m on the SkiErg, a 50 m sled push, a 50 m sled pull, 80 m of burpee broad jumps, 1,000 m of rowing, a 200 m farmers carry, 100 m of sandbag lunges and 100 wall balls. Elite men finish inside an hour; the average finisher is closer to ninety minutes. Divisions cover open, pro (heavier sleds and wall balls), doubles and relays, so the same event holds first-timers and full-time athletes on the same course.</p>\n<h2>Why it grew this fast</h2>\n<p>The first race in Hamburg in 2017 drew 650 athletes; the 2025/26 season projects well over a million participants across more than a hundred events in thirty-plus countries, with races like New York selling out in under an hour. The fixed format is the growth engine: no technical Olympic lifts, no judge-dependent movements, no course variation &mdash; just running and eight exercises most gym-goers already know, with a finish time that means the same thing in every city. The overview and format details are on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyrox">Wikipedia&rsquo;s Hyrox entry</a>.</p>\n<h2>What the race actually demands</h2>\n<p>Roughly half the winning time is running, which surprises people who trained the stations hardest. The sled work and lunges are strength-endurance; the SkiErg and rower are full-body ergometers; the wall balls at the end, after 7 km of racing, are a pacing trap. A sensible preparation splits accordingly: a durable aerobic base &mdash; the same machinery described in <a href="/fitness/zone-2-cardio-explained/">zone 2 cardio explained</a> &mdash; plus station-specific practice, because sled pushing and wall balls are skills as much as fitness. Interval work of the kind in <a href="/fitness/hiit-training-explained/">HIIT training explained</a> maps well onto the run-station rhythm.</p>\n<h2>Who it suits</h2>\n<p>Runners who want a reason to train strength, and lifters who want a reason to train endurance, are the two natural converts &mdash; Hyrox is the race that finally punishes one-sided training equally. The comparison with the other big functional-fitness brand is in <a href="/fitness/hyrox-vs-crossfit-explained/">Hyrox versus CrossFit</a>, and if the running half is your weak side, carrying load over distance is a bridge worth building first &mdash; see <a href="/fitness/rucking-explained/">rucking explained</a>.</p>'),

('hyrox-vs-crossfit-explained', 'Compare', 'Hyrox versus CrossFit: two functional fitness systems, honestly compared', 'Fixed race versus varied programming, running versus gymnastics, mass-start events versus the Open - which system suits which athlete, and where they overlap.', '<p>Hyrox and CrossFit get lumped together as &ldquo;functional fitness&rdquo;, and they do share a population. But they are different products with different demands, and the choice between them is really a choice about what you want training to be.</p>\n<h2>The core structural difference</h2>\n<p>Hyrox is a <b>race with a fixed format</b>: the same 8 km and eight stations everywhere, which makes it a time trial against everyone who has ever run it. CrossFit is a <b>training methodology</b> built on constantly varied functional movements performed at high intensity &mdash; the daily workout changes, and competition (the Open, the Games) is a separate layer on top of the training. One gives you a repeatable benchmark; the other gives you a varied practice. Both are documented at length: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyrox">Hyrox</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CrossFit">CrossFit</a> on Wikipedia.</p>\n<h2>What each one demands physically</h2>\n<p>Hyrox is roughly half running plus eight gym stations &mdash; ergometers, sleds, carries, lunges, wall balls. No Olympic lifts, no gymnastics rings, no movements that take years to acquire. CrossFit programming regularly includes snatches, cleans, muscle-ups and handstand work: higher skill ceiling, broader physical variety, and a longer runway before you are competitive in the workouts as written. If you have a running background, Hyrox rewards you on day one; if you have a gymnastics or weightlifting background, CrossFit does.</p>\n<h2>The culture and access difference</h2>\n<p>CrossFit is delivered through affiliated boxes with coaches and a class structure; Hyrox is delivered as mass-participation race events, with training left to you or your gym. That means CrossFit answers &ldquo;how should I train this week&rdquo; by default, while Hyrox answers &ldquo;what am I training for&rdquo; &mdash; many people now use CrossFit-style or general gym programming to prepare for a Hyrox race. The interval machinery both rely on is covered in <a href="/fitness/hiit-training-explained/">HIIT training explained</a>.</p>\n<h2>Which should you pick?</h2>\n<p>Want a coach, a community and variety as the product itself? CrossFit. Want a number to chase, a race calendar and the freedom to train however you like? Hyrox &mdash; the format is in <a href="/fitness/hyrox-explained/">Hyrox explained</a>. Plenty of athletes do both: the strength work that makes CrossFit interesting is the same work that fixes a Hyrox sled split, and the aerobic base is shared. Whichever you choose, the recovery arithmetic is identical &mdash; see <a href="/fitness/rest-days-and-recovery/">rest days and recovery</a>.</p>'),

('what-does-3x10-mean-explained', 'Understand', 'What does 3x10 mean? Reading a workout programme for the first time', 'Sets, reps, load and the unstated assumptions behind every number on a training card - plus how to choose the weight and when to change the numbers.', '<p>Every training programme eventually hands you a line like <b>3x10</b> and moves on. It is the most common notation in lifting and the least often explained, so here is the full translation, including the parts programmes leave out.</p>\n<h2>The basic translation</h2>\n<p>3x10 means <b>three sets of ten repetitions</b>: perform the exercise ten times, rest, repeat, three times through. The first number is sets, the second is reps. 4x8 is four sets of eight; 3x8&ndash;12 is three sets of a rep range, meaning stop the set somewhere between eight and twelve honest reps. If a weight is prescribed it appears alongside (3x10 @ 60 kg); if not, you choose it, and that choice is the real content of the line.</p>\n<h2>Choosing the weight: the part nobody writes down</h2>\n<p>The number that makes 3x10 work is effort, not arithmetic. Pick a load where the eighth, ninth and tenth reps are genuinely hard but technically clean &mdash; roughly two to three reps short of failure on the last set. Too light and three sets of ten is a warm-up; too heavy and the tenth rep is a negotiation with gravity. The effort scales that make this precise are in <a href="/fitness/rpe-and-rir-explained/">RPE and RIR explained</a>, and why the rep range itself matters &mdash; what ten reps trains versus five or twenty &mdash; is in <a href="/fitness/how-many-reps-for-muscle/">how many reps for muscle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rest, and the numbers you will see next</h2>\n<p>Programmes rarely print rest periods, but three sets of ten on a compound lift usually wants two to three minutes between sets; isolation work manages on sixty to ninety seconds. The reasoning is in <a href="/fitness/rest-between-sets-explained/">rest between sets explained</a>. As programmes advance, 3x10 mutates: into double progressions (add reps to the top of the range, then add load), into wave loading, into tempo prescriptions &mdash; the vocabulary in <a href="/fitness/gym-acronyms-explained/">gym acronyms decoded</a> covers most of what you will meet.</p>\n<h2>When 3x10 stops being the right line</h2>\n<p>Three sets of ten is a default, not a law. It works because it sits in the middle of the useful load spectrum for beginners and intermediates on most exercises. It stops being right when the goal moves &mdash; maximal strength wants heavier loads and fewer reps, endurance wants more of both &mdash; or when progress stalls, which is the cue to change the stimulus rather than suffer through it. The full menu of what to change is in <a href="/fitness/how-progressive-overload-works/">how progressive overload works</a>.</p>'),

('supersets-vs-circuits-explained', 'Train', 'Supersets versus circuits: what the structures actually change', 'Two exercises back to back versus a whole loop of them - the strength cost, the time saving, and which structure suits which goal, according to what the research supports.', '<p>Supersets and circuits both compress rest, and both get sold as fat-loss magic. They are structures, not metabolisms: what they change is density &mdash; how much work fits into the time you have &mdash; and the trade-off is that density has a cost when strength is the goal.</p>\n<h2>The definitions, precisely</h2>\n<p>A <b>superset</b> is two exercises performed back to back with no rest between them, then a normal rest before the pair repeats. A <b>circuit</b> is three or more exercises cycled through with minimal rest, the whole loop repeated for rounds. A tri-set is a superset of three; a giant set is a superset of four or more. The terms get used loosely in class settings &mdash; most &ldquo;circuit classes&rdquo; are timed stations, which is a different structure again.</p>\n<h2>What they genuinely save, and what they cost</h2>\n<p>The saving is real: supersets and circuits routinely cut session time by a third or more, which matters because adherence is the first variable in any programme. The cost depends on the pairing. Superset the <b>same muscle group</b> and the second exercise is performed under accumulated fatigue &mdash; fewer quality reps at the same load, which is a strength cost disguised as intensity. Superset <b>opposing muscle groups</b> &mdash; a row paired with a press, a curl with an extension &mdash; and the cost mostly disappears, because each muscle rests while the other works. That antagonist pairing is the version the evidence treats kindly.</p>\n<h2>Which structure suits which goal</h2>\n<p>For <b>maximum strength</b> on a main lift, neither: take your full rest, as argued in <a href="/fitness/rest-between-sets-explained/">rest between sets explained</a>. For <b>muscle growth</b> with limited time, antagonist supersets and moderate circuits preserve most of the stimulus at higher density. For <b>general fitness and conditioning</b>, circuits win outright &mdash; the continuous structure is cardio with a barbell in it, and the metabolic demand of the format is what <a href="/fitness/hiit-training-explained/">HIIT training explained</a> formalises with intervals. For <b>fat loss</b>, the honest answer is that the structure matters less than total weekly activity and diet; density helps by making the session short enough to actually attend.</p>\n<h2>Programming them without wrecking the week</h2>\n<p>Two rules keep supersets and circuits productive. First, keep your heaviest, most technical lifts outside them &mdash; fatigue plus a complex movement is where form breaks. Second, treat them as the density layer of a programme, not the whole programme: a main lift, then superset or circuit work, then done. The adjacent intensity techniques &mdash; doing more with the same session &mdash; are catalogued in <a href="/fitness/drop-sets-and-rest-pause-explained/">drop sets and rest-pause explained</a>, and the soreness these formats produce deserves the same reading as any other: <a href="/fitness/muscle-soreness-vs-injury/">soreness versus injury</a>.</p>'),

('12-3-30-workout-explained', 'Train', 'The 12-3-30 workout, explained: incline walking past the hype', 'What the treadmill numbers mean, why the format stuck, what it genuinely trains, and the three limits that make it a workout rather than a programme.', '<p>Twelve percent incline, three miles per hour, thirty minutes: the treadmill workout that outlived its own trend cycle. It spread because it is easy to describe, and it stuck because underneath the hashtag it is a legitimate, low-impact steady-state session &mdash; with limits worth naming.</p>\n<h2>What the numbers are</h2>\n<p>Set the treadmill to a <b>12% grade</b>, walk at <b>3 mph</b> (about 4.8 km/h), and stay there for <b>30 minutes</b>. That is the whole prescription. The steep grade converts flat walking into a climbing effort: heart rate settles into a sustained moderate-to-vigorous band without running, and the posterior chain &mdash; calves, hamstrings, glutes &mdash; does meaningfully more work than level walking. The intensity is roughly comparable to a steady jog for many people, at a fraction of the impact.</p>\n<h2>Why it works when it works</h2>\n<p>Three mechanisms, all boring in the best way. It is <b>sustainable</b>: thirty minutes at conversational-plus effort is an intensity people repeat three times a week without dread, and repetition is where fitness is actually made. It is <b>low-impact</b>: no flight phase means joints see walking forces, which opens aerobic training to heavier beginners and returners from injury. And it is <b>honest zone-2-adjacent work</b>: the same aerobic machinery built by <a href="/fitness/zone-2-cardio-explained/">zone 2 cardio explained</a>, with the incline doing what speed would otherwise do. Carrying weight over distance is the outdoor cousin of the same stimulus &mdash; <a href="/fitness/rucking-explained/">rucking explained</a> covers it.</p>\n<h2>The three honest limits</h2>\n<p>First, it is <b>one workout, not a programme</b>: no resistance training means no strength or muscle stimulus, and no variation means no progressive overload &mdash; the principle in <a href="/fitness/how-progressive-overload-works/">how progressive overload works</a> eventually asks for more than the same thirty minutes. Second, the <b>calf and Achilles load</b> is real: a sustained 12% grade is a lot of plantar-flexion work for anyone new to inclines, and ramping up too fast is the classic injury route &mdash; start at 5&ndash;8% and build. Third, hand-holding the rails quietly deletes much of the work; if you need the rails at 12%, the honest fix is a lower grade.</p>\n<h2>Who it suits</h2>\n<p>Beginners building a base, returning athletes, heavier trainees protecting joints, and anyone whose alternative to a simple, repeatable session is no session. For fitter athletes it works best as easy-day aerobic volume or a finisher &mdash; the high-intensity contrast is what <a href="/fitness/hiit-training-explained/">HIIT training explained</a> provides. Judge it like any tool: by whether you did it consistently, not by what the internet promised it would do in two weeks.</p>'),

('weighted-vests-explained', 'Kit', 'Weighted vests, explained: when extra load earns its place', 'What a vest actually changes about walking, lifting and bodyweight work, the evidence on bones and aerobic cost, and the safety rules that keep loaded movement a good idea.', '<p>A weighted vest is the simplest idea in training equipment &mdash; make bodyweight heavier &mdash; and like most simple ideas it has a useful range and a foolish one. The useful range is wider than the marketing suggests; the foolish one is shorter than the marketing admits.</p>\n<h2>What the vest actually changes</h2>\n<p>Adding 5&ndash;10% of bodyweight to walking raises the energy cost of every step proportionally, which is why loaded walking is a recognised way to build aerobic demand without speed &mdash; the same logic that makes <a href="/fitness/rucking-explained/">rucking</a> effective, with the load distributed more evenly than a backpack manages. On bodyweight strength work &mdash; push-ups, pull-ups, dips, squats &mdash; the vest is a progressive-overload tool for people who have outgrown their own weight, slotted into the same menu as the levers in <a href="/fitness/progressive-overload-without-adding-weight-explained/">progressive overload without adding weight</a>. The equipment category and its history are covered on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weighted_clothing">Wikipedia&rsquo;s weighted clothing entry</a>.</p>\n<h2>The bone argument</h2>\n<p>The most durable claim is skeletal: bone adapts to load, and adding mass to weight-bearing activity increases the mechanical stimulus per step. Research on loaded walking supports modest improvements in bone-mineral measures, strongest in the populations that need them most &mdash; post-menopausal and older adults &mdash; which is why loaded walking features in the bone-density discussion in <a href="/fitness/exercise-for-bone-density-explained/">exercise for bone density explained</a>. The vest is not a substitute for resistance training; it makes the walking you already do count for more.</p>\n<h2>The safety rules that matter</h2>\n<p>Start light &mdash; 5% of bodyweight is a real dose, not a warm-up &mdash; and build the distance before the load. Running in a vest deserves extra caution: impact forces scale with the added mass on every stride, so it is a tool for trained runners with a specific reason, not a default upgrade. Anyone with back, hip or knee symptoms should treat loaded walking as something to clear with a professional first, and any sharp or joint-localised signal gets the honest read in <a href="/fitness/muscle-soreness-vs-injury/">soreness versus injury</a> before the next session.</p>\n<h2>What to buy, and what to skip</h2>\n<p>Two features matter: <b>adjustability</b> &mdash; removable blocks let the same vest serve walking at 5% and pull-ups at 15% &mdash; and <b>fit</b>, because a vest that shifts or slaps the shoulders turns every step into a distraction. Short, torso-length designs behave better under a barbell; long designs suit walking. Everything beyond that is branding. Used inside its range &mdash; walking, bodyweight work, occasional conditioning &mdash; a vest is one of the few pieces of kit that quietly compounds; used as a shortcut to harder everything, it is a fast route to an overuse complaint.</p>'),

]

